eval("__webpack_require__.r(__webpack_exports__);\n/* harmony export */ __webpack_require__.d(__webpack_exports__, {\n/* harmony export */   articles: () => (/* binding */ articles)\n/* harmony export */ });\nconst articles = [\n    {\n        id: \"1\",\n        slug: \"red-light-therapy-ultimate-guide\",\n        title: \"A Beginner's Guide to LED Light Therapy: What You Should Know Before Buying\",\n        seoTitle: \"LED Light Therapy Guide 2026 | What to Know Before You Buy\",\n        seoDescription: \"Considering an LED light therapy mask? This guide covers how LED devices work, what different colours do, key features to compare, and what to look for in the UK market.\",\n        seoKeywords: \"led light therapy guide, led mask buyer guide, red light therapy explained, led mask uk\",\n        author: \"Trustpilot Review Shop Editorial\",\n        date: \"March 10, 2026\",\n        image: \"https://img.thesitebase.net/10677/10677322/themes/177107744580dd01d13d.png\",\n        excerpt: \"LED light therapy has grown rapidly in popularity over the past few years. But with so many devices on the market, where do you start? This guide walks through the basics of how LED masks work, what each colour is designed for, and what features matter most when you are shopping for one.\",\n        content: [\n            \"LED light therapy has been available in professional skin care clinics for many years, and over the past decade, at-home devices have become increasingly accessible. If you have been scrolling through social media or browsing beauty retailers, you have probably seen those glowing face masks popping up everywhere. But what exactly are they, and are they worth your money?\",\n            \"At a basic level, LED (Light Emitting Diode) therapy involves exposing your skin to specific wavelengths of light. Different wavelengths correspond to different colours, and each colour is associated with different properties. For example, red light (typically around 630nm) is one of the most commonly used wavelengths. According to various published studies, red light wavelengths may help support the skin's natural renewal processes. Near-infrared light (around 850nm) penetrates deeper and is often used in wellness applications.\",\n            \"Blue light (around 415nm) is another popular wavelength. It is often discussed in the context of skin care routines that target blemish-prone skin. Green light (525nm) and yellow light (590nm) are less common but are included in some multi-colour devices. Each colour has its own set of properties that manufacturers highlight, though individual experiences can vary significantly from person to person.\",\n            \"One of the key things to understand before buying an LED mask is the difference between single-colour and multi-colour devices. A single-colour mask will only emit one wavelength, which limits its versatility. Multi-colour masks, which offer anywhere from 3 to 7 different light settings, give you more flexibility to adjust your routine based on your personal preferences and seasonal changes in your skin.\",\n            \"Coverage area is another important factor that many buyers overlook. Most LED masks on the market cover only the face. However, some newer models have extended panels that also cover the neck and jawline area. If even coverage is important to you, it is worth paying attention to whether the mask extends below the chin or if a separate attachment is required, which can significantly increase the total cost.\",\n            \"Material and comfort matter more than you might think, especially if you plan to use the device regularly. Rigid plastic masks can feel heavy and uncomfortable during longer sessions, while flexible silicone masks tend to conform better to different face shapes. Weight, ventilation, and whether the device is cordless are all practical considerations that affect how consistently you will actually use it.\",\n            \"Safety certifications should be a key consideration before purchasing. Look for devices that have been tested and certified — common certifications include the CE marking (required for products sold in the UK and EU). Some manufacturers also state that their devices have attained FDA clearance in the US, which indicates they have submitted documentation regarding safety and efficacy for specific uses. These certifications are part of a manufacturer's compliance process and help verify that the device meets defined safety thresholds.\",\n            \"Price is obviously a major consideration. LED masks range from under £30 to well over £500. Generally speaking, cheaper devices tend to use lower-quality LEDs that may not emit the precise wavelengths claimed. Mid-range and premium devices typically use higher-quality components and have undergone more rigorous testing. That said, the most expensive option is not automatically the best — it pays to compare specifications rather than just price tags.\",\n            \"When comparing specific models, pay attention to the number of LEDs, the stated wavelengths, the treatment area, battery life (if cordless), and what is included in the box. Some brands include carrying cases, eye protection, and detailed usage instructions, while others provide just the bare minimum. User reviews on independent platforms can also give you a more realistic picture of what to expect.\",\n            \"In the UK market specifically, some of the well-known brands include CurrentBody, Omnilux, and Buudy. Each positions itself slightly differently — some emphasise brand heritage and clinical associations, while others focus on value for money by including more features at a lower price point. We have reviewed many of these devices on our site, so feel free to explore our other articles for detailed comparisons.\",\n            \"Ultimately, the right LED mask for you depends on your budget, your priorities, and how much flexibility you want in your routine. There is no single 'best' device for everyone. We recommend making a shortlist based on the features that matter most to you, reading multiple reviews, and checking return policies before committing to a purchase.\"\n        ]\n    },\n    {\n        id: \"2\",\n        slug: \"blue-red-light-blemish-prone-skin\",\n        title: \"Blue and Red Light for Blemish-Prone Skin: A Practical Overview\",\n        seoTitle: \"LED Light Therapy for Blemish-Prone Skin | Blue & Red Light Guide\",\n        seoDescription: \"Exploring LED light therapy for blemish-prone skin? Learn how blue and red wavelengths are used in home LED masks, what to expect, and how to choose the right device.\",\n        seoKeywords: \"led mask for blemishes, blue light therapy skin, red light blemishes, led face mask acne uk\",\n        author: \"Trustpilot Review Shop Editorial\",\n        date: \"March 5, 2026\",\n        image: \"https://img.thesitebase.net/10677/10677322/themes/176943060543a303d043.png?width=828&height=0&min_height=0\",\n        excerpt: \"If you have blemish-prone skin, you may have heard about LED light therapy as a non-chemical approach worth exploring. Here is a straightforward look at what blue and red LED light are commonly used for, and what to consider when choosing a device.\",\n        content: [\n            \"Dealing with blemish-prone skin as an adult can be frustrating. Many people have tried various topical products with mixed results, and the idea of a light-based approach that does not involve chemicals can sound appealing. LED light therapy has gained attention as one such option, particularly devices that use blue and red wavelengths together.\",\n            \"Blue LED light, typically around 415nm, is one of the most studied wavelengths in the context of skin care. Research has explored its properties related to certain types of bacteria on the skin's surface. Many LED mask manufacturers highlight blue light as a key feature for people who want to incorporate it into their skin care routine alongside their existing products.\",\n            \"Red LED light (around 630nm) serves a different purpose. It is generally associated with supporting the skin's natural recovery processes. When skin is dealing with blemishes, redness and irritation often accompany the breakouts. Red light is included in many devices as a complementary wavelength to use alongside or alternating with blue light sessions.\",\n            \"It is worth noting that at-home LED light therapy is a supplementary approach and not a replacement for advice from a qualified dermatologist or doctor. If you have persistent skin concerns, a professional consultation should be your first step to ensure you receive a proper diagnosis. At-home LED masks are designed to be used as part of a broader routine for general skin wellness.\",\n            \"When shopping for a device specifically for blemish-prone skin, make sure the mask actually includes blue light. This might seem obvious, but some popular 'anti-aging' focused masks only include red and near-infrared wavelengths. If blue light is important to your routine, check the product specifications carefully before purchasing.\",\n            \"Multi-colour devices offer the most flexibility. With a 7-colour mask, for example, you can alternate between different light settings on different days based on how your skin is behaving. Some people prefer using blue light several times a week and red light on other days, though optimal routines vary from person to person.\",\n            \"Consistency appears to be a common factor among users who report positive experiences. Unlike a single clinic visit, at-home devices are designed for regular use over weeks and months. This is why comfort and convenience matter so much — if the mask is uncomfortable or requires a complicated setup, you are less likely to stick with it.\",\n            \"The UK market offers several options in this category. Devices from brands like Buudy, CurrentBody, and Omnilux all include some form of blue and red light capability, though they differ in terms of wavelength count, coverage area, and price. Buudy, for instance, includes 7 different colour options and extends coverage to the neck area, while some competitors focus only on the face with fewer wavelength options.\",\n            \"Budget is a real consideration here. Some devices cost over £400 and may require additional accessories to cover the neck or chest. Others offer comparable specifications at a lower price point. We always recommend comparing the specific features side by side rather than assuming higher price equals better performance.\",\n            \"In summary, if you are curious about incorporating LED light therapy into your routine for blemish-prone skin, a device with both blue and red wavelengths gives you the most relevant options. Look for a comfortable, well-certified device within your budget, be consistent with use, and maintain realistic expectations about what any at-home device can deliver.\"\n        ]\n    },\n    {\n        id: \"3\",\n        slug: \"led-masks-for-mature-skin\",\n        title: \"LED Masks and Mature Skin: What the Research Says and What to Consider\",\n        seoTitle: \"LED Mask for Mature Skin UK | Red & Infrared Light Review\",\n        seoDescription: \"Interested in using an LED mask as part of your mature skin care routine? Here is what published research says about red and infrared light, and practical tips for choosing a device.\",\n        seoKeywords: \"led mask for mature skin, led mask wrinkles, red light mature skin care, infrared light skin uk\",\n        author: \"Trustpilot Review Shop Editorial\",\n        date: \"February 28, 2026\",\n        image: \"https://lawngreen-kingfisher-468763.hostingersite.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/57-w-1.webp\",\n        excerpt: \"Red and near-infrared LED light have been the subject of numerous studies exploring their effects on skin. If you have mature skin and are considering adding an LED mask to your routine, here is a balanced look at what is out there.\",\n        content: [\n            \"As we get older, our skin naturally changes. Collagen production slows, the skin becomes thinner, and fine lines become more noticeable. These are completely normal biological processes. Many people look for ways to support their skin as it changes, and LED light therapy has become one of the options that people explore.\",\n            \"Red light therapy (around 630nm) has been studied in various contexts related to skin health. Some peer-reviewed studies have reported that consistent exposure to red light wavelengths may support the skin's natural collagen production processes. However, it is important to note that study conditions often differ significantly from how people use at-home devices, and results reported in clinical settings do not automatically translate to identical outcomes at home.\",\n            \"Near-infrared light (around 850nm) penetrates deeper into tissue than visible red light. It is commonly used in physiotherapy and wellness contexts. Some LED mask manufacturers include near-infrared LEDs in their devices, positioning them as useful for people interested in supporting deeper tissue layers. As with red light, published research exists but individual experiences vary.\",\n            \"If you decide to try LED therapy as part of your mature skin care routine, it is best used as a complement to your existing regimen—not as a replacement. A solid routine that includes sun protection, appropriate moisturising, and professional check-ups remains the foundation of any good skin care approach, regardless of age.\",\n            \"When evaluating LED masks for mature skin specifically, red and near-infrared wavelengths are the ones most commonly discussed. However, some users also appreciate having access to other colours. For example, yellow light (590nm) is sometimes included in multi-colour devices and is associated with soothing properties. Green light (525nm) is another wavelength that some manufacturers include for users interested in addressing uneven skin tone.\",\n            \"Coverage area becomes particularly relevant for mature skin care. The neck and chest areas often show signs of aging before the face does, yet most LED masks stop at the jawline. If comprehensive coverage is a priority, look for devices that include an integrated neck panel rather than one sold separately as an expensive add-on.\",\n            \"Comfort and session length matter, especially for regular use. Most manufacturers recommend sessions of 10 to 20 minutes. A lightweight, flexible mask that you can wear while reading or relaxing will make it much easier to maintain a consistent routine compared to a rigid, heavy device that feels like a chore to put on.\",\n            \"In the UK, popular options include the CurrentBody LED mask (known for its flexible design), the Omnilux Contour (positioned as a premium option), and the Buudy 7-Colour LED Mask (which includes neck coverage and multiple wavelengths at a mid-range price point). Each has its strengths and trade-offs, which we cover in more detail in our comparison reviews.\",\n            \"Price ranges vary considerably. Some devices are priced above £400 and focus on brand prestige and clinical associations. Others, like the Buudy mask at £179, aim to offer a similar feature set — including 7 colours and neck coverage — at a more accessible price. Whether the premium brands justify their higher price tags is a personal judgment that depends on your budget and priorities.\",\n            \"Ultimately, adding an LED mask to your routine is a personal choice based on your goals. While there is promising research in this area, individual results can vary and at-home devices should be viewed as part of a long-term approach to skin wellness rather than a guaranteed fix. It is always best to set realistic expectations and prioritise professional advice for any specific skin conditions.\"\n        ]\n    },\n    {\n        id: \"4\",\n        slug: \"led-mask-face-neck-coverage-guide\",\n        title: \"Why Neck Coverage Matters When Choosing an LED Mask\",\n        seoTitle: \"LED Mask with Neck Coverage UK | Face & Neck Comparison\",\n        seoDescription: \"Many LED masks only cover the face. Here is why neck coverage is worth considering, which devices include it, and how to avoid paying extra for separate attachments.\",\n        seoKeywords: \"led mask with neck coverage, led mask face and neck, neck led therapy uk, full coverage led mask\",\n        author: \"Trustpilot Review Shop Editorial\",\n        date: \"February 15, 2026\",\n        image: \"https://img.thesitebase.net/10677/10677322/themes/176872504642f0322d65.jpeg\",\n        excerpt: \"When shopping for an LED mask, one detail that many buyers overlook is coverage area. Most masks stop at the jawline, but the neck is often the area that could benefit most from attention. Here is what to consider.\",\n        content: [\n            \"If you are in the market for an LED face mask, you have probably noticed that the vast majority of devices on the market cover only the face — from forehead to chin. This seems logical at first glance, but many skin care professionals point out that the neck and upper chest are areas where skin changes often become noticeable first, particularly as we get older.\",\n            \"The skin on the neck is structurally different from facial skin. It tends to be thinner, has fewer oil glands, and is subject to constant movement. In our modern era of smartphones and laptops, many people spend hours looking down at screens, which can contribute to the development of horizontal lines on the neck — sometimes referred to informally as 'tech neck' lines.\",\n            \"For many people, having a consistent skin care approach that includes the neck area makes practical sense. If you are already investing in an LED device for your face, extending that same routine to your neck seems like a natural step. The challenge is that most manufacturers either do not offer neck coverage at all, or sell it as a separate attachment that can cost an additional £200 to £300.\",\n            \"This pricing structure can feel frustrating. You invest in a premium face mask, only to discover that the matching neck piece costs nearly as much as the original device. Some buyers report feeling that this approach feels more like upselling than genuine product design. Of course, there are also practical reasons why manufacturers may separate the components — adding neck coverage increases manufacturing complexity and device weight.\",\n            \"Some newer brands have taken a different approach by integrating the neck panel directly into the main mask design. The Buudy 7-Colour LED Mask is one example — it extends below the jawline to cover the neck area with the same LED array used on the face, all included in a single device at a single price point. This integrated approach has its own trade-offs (the mask is slightly larger), but it does eliminate the need for a separate purchase.\",\n            \"When evaluating devices with neck coverage, pay attention to whether the neck section uses the same quality of LEDs, the same wavelengths, and the same density of bulbs as the face section. Some devices technically include a neck attachment but use fewer or lower-quality LEDs in that area, which could result in uneven exposure.\",\n            \"Fit and comfort are particularly important for masks that include neck coverage, since a larger device needs to sit securely without sliding around. Adjustable straps and flexible materials help ensure that the mask maintains contact with the skin across both the face and neck throughout the session.\",\n            \"In terms of pricing in the UK market, face-only LED masks from premium brands like CurrentBody and Omnilux typically range from £300 to £500 for the face mask alone. Adding a neck attachment can push the total investment to £600 or more. The Buudy mask, at £179 with integrated neck coverage, represents a different value proposition that some buyers find compelling.\",\n            \"Of course, price is not the only factor. Brand reputation, build quality, warranty terms, and customer service all play a role in the purchase decision. We always recommend reading multiple reviews and comparing specifications before making a final choice.\",\n            \"In summary, if neck coverage is important to you, check whether it is included in the base price or requires an additional purchase. Consider how the device fits, whether the LED quality is consistent across all panels, and whether the total investment aligns with your budget. Our comparison reviews cover these details for all the major brands available in the UK.\"\n        ]\n    },\n    {\n        id: \"5\",\n        slug: \"choosing-safe-led-mask\",\n        title: \"How to Choose a Safe LED Mask: Certifications, Materials, and Red Flags\",\n        seoTitle: \"Safe LED Mask Guide UK | Certifications & What to Avoid\",\n        seoDescription: \"Not all LED masks are created equal. Learn about safety certifications, material quality, and warning signs to watch for when shopping for an LED light therapy device.\",\n        seoKeywords: \"safe led mask uk, ce certified led mask, led mask safety, how to choose led mask\",\n        author: \"Trustpilot Review Shop Editorial\",\n        date: \"February 2, 2026\",\n        image: \"https://img.thesitebase.net/10677/10677322/themes/1769107230af732ce69a.jpeg\",\n        excerpt: \"With LED masks available at every price point from £20 to £500+, safety becomes an important consideration. Here is how to evaluate an LED mask's safety credentials, what certifications to look for, and what red flags should make you think twice.\",\n        content: [\n            \"The LED mask market has grown rapidly over the past few years, and with that growth has come an influx of products at every price point. While many devices are well-made and properly tested, the sheer volume of options means that sub-standard products inevitably find their way onto the market. Knowing how to evaluate safety is an important part of making a smart purchase.\",\n            \"The most important thing to check is whether the device has relevant safety certifications. In the UK and EU, look for CE marking, which indicates compliance with applicable safety directives. You may also see some products mentioned as being FDA-cleared; this is a US standard indicating that the manufacturer has met specific regulatory requirements. These certifications provide a baseline of assurance that the device has been designed with safety in mind.\",\n            \"Material quality is another consideration. LED masks sit directly on your face for extended periods, so the materials should be skin-safe and non-irritating. Medical-grade silicone is considered one of the best options because it is hypoallergenic, flexible, and comfortable. Rigid plastic masks may be less comfortable and can create pressure points during longer sessions.\",\n            \"Pay attention to eye protection. LED light, particularly at higher intensities, can be uncomfortable or potentially harmful to the eyes with prolonged exposure. Quality devices typically include built-in eye protection, opaque eye shields, or separate goggles. If a device makes no mention of eye safety, that is a significant red flag.\",\n            \"Battery safety is relevant for cordless devices. Lithium-ion batteries, while generally safe, need to be properly engineered with appropriate thermal management. Very cheap devices may cut corners on battery quality, which in extreme cases could lead to overheating. Look for devices from established brands that provide clear information about their battery specifications and safety testing.\",\n            \"Be cautious of devices that make grandiose claims. A product that promises to 'reverse aging in 7 days' or 'permanently clear your skin' should be viewed with scepticism. Reputable brands tend to be more measured in their claims, referencing general wellness benefits or citing specific studies rather than making sweeping promises. Google Ads policies also penalise websites that make unsubstantiated health claims, which is why responsible brands avoid such language.\",\n            \"User reviews on independent platforms can provide valuable real-world insights. Look for reviews that discuss long-term use, comfort, and customer service experiences. Be aware that some brands incentivise positive reviews, so try to read across multiple platforms for a balanced perspective.\",\n            \"Warranty and return policies are worth checking before you buy. A brand that stands behind its product will typically offer at least a one-year warranty and a reasonable return window. If a device has no warranty or a restrictive return policy, that may indicate less confidence in the product's durability.\",\n            \"In the UK market, devices from CurrentBody, Omnilux, and Buudy all carry appropriate CE certifications and provide clear information about their safety credentials. They differ in other ways — pricing, features, and design — but all meet basic safety thresholds. Budget devices from unknown brands on marketplace sites may not offer the same level of assurance.\",\n            \"In short, do not buy an LED mask based solely on price or marketing. Check certifications, read the fine print on material quality and eye safety, look at independent reviews, and buy from brands that provide transparent product information. Your face is not the place to cut corners on quality.\"\n        ]\n    },\n    {\n        id: \"6\",\n        slug: \"understanding-led-light-colours\",\n        title: \"Understanding LED Light Colours: What Each Wavelength Is Used For\",\n        seoTitle: \"LED Mask Colours Explained | What Does Each Light Do?\",\n        seoDescription: \"Wondering what green, yellow, or cyan LED light does? This guide explains the 7 most common LED wavelengths used in face masks and what each colour is typically associated with.\",\n        seoKeywords: \"led mask colours explained, green light therapy, yellow light skin, 7 colour led mask, led wavelengths\",\n        author: \"Trustpilot Review Shop Editorial\",\n        date: \"January 20, 2026\",\n        image: \"https://img.thesitebase.net/10677/10677322/themes/1768726434a7e6301df7.png\",\n        excerpt: \"LED masks come with different colour options, but what does each colour actually do? Here is a plain-English breakdown of the 7 most common LED wavelengths and what each one is typically associated with in skin care contexts.\",\n        content: [\n            \"If you have looked at LED face masks, you have probably noticed that they come with different colour options. Some masks offer just one or two colours, while others include up to seven. But what is the difference between red and blue? What about green or yellow? Here is a straightforward guide to help you understand what each wavelength is commonly used for.\",\n            \"Red light (approximately 630nm) is the most widely used and most studied wavelength in LED skin care. It is commonly associated with supporting the skin's natural renewal processes. Many people who use red light report that their skin feels smoother over time with consistent use. Red light is featured in virtually every LED mask on the market, from budget options to premium devices.\",\n            \"Near-infrared light (approximately 850nm) is not visible to the human eye, but it is included in many LED masks. It penetrates deeper than visible red light and is commonly used in physiotherapy and wellness contexts. In the context of LED masks, near-infrared is often positioned as a complement to red light for a more comprehensive routine.\",\n            \"Blue light (approximately 415nm) is the second most popular wavelength. It is frequently discussed in relation to blemish-prone skin. Many LED mask manufacturers include blue light as a standard feature, and it is one of the more well-researched wavelengths in the context of dermatological applications.\",\n            \"Green light (approximately 525nm) is less commonly discussed but is included in many multi-colour devices. It is typically associated with addressing uneven skin tone and is highlighted by some manufacturers as useful for those dealing with sun spots or pigmentation concerns. Research in this area is less extensive than for red or blue light.\",\n            \"Yellow light (approximately 590nm) sits between red and green on the spectrum. It is often described as a soothing wavelength and is associated with reducing the appearance of redness. Some users with sensitive skin report finding yellow light sessions comfortable and calming, though individual experiences naturally vary.\",\n            \"Cyan light (approximately 490nm) falls between blue and green. It is one of the less common wavelengths and is not included in all multi-colour masks. Manufacturers that include it sometimes describe it as a multipurpose wavelength that combines some properties of both blue and green light.\",\n            \"Purple or violet light is sometimes included as a combination of red and blue wavelengths emitted simultaneously. The idea is to offer some of the properties associated with both colours in a single session. Not all masks include this as a separate option — some achieve a similar effect by simply running red and blue LEDs at the same time.\",\n            \"When choosing a multi-colour LED mask, consider which wavelengths are most relevant to your interests. If you are primarily interested in general skin wellness and renewal, red and near-infrared may be sufficient. If you want more versatility for different uses, a 7-colour device gives you the most options. Keep in mind that having more colour options does not automatically make a device better — the quality of the LEDs and the precision of the wavelengths matter just as much.\",\n            \"In the UK, the Buudy 7-Colour LED Mask offers all seven wavelengths we have discussed here, plus neck coverage, at £179. CurrentBody offers a popular red/near-infrared device that focuses on fewer wavelengths but at a premium build quality. Omnilux provides clinical positioning with a focus on red and near-infrared. Each approach has merit depending on your priorities and budget.\",\n            \"We hope this guide helps demystify the colour options available in LED masks. As always, we recommend doing your own research, reading multiple reviews, and consulting a professional if you have specific skin concerns before making a purchase decision.\"\n        ]\n    }\n];\n//# sourceURL=[module]\n//# sourceMappingURL=data:application/json;charset=utf-8;base64,\n//# sourceURL=webpack-internal:///(rsc)/./src/data/old/articles.ts\n");

